This is a local gem5 fork used by the qflex/BXKraken checkpoint-conversion
project.

It should not be read as a generic upstream gem5 checkout with no special
purpose. In this repository, gem5 is the timing-simulation backend for
checkpoints created from BXKraken-origin source state and prepared through the
surrounding qflex and QPoints workflow.

The maintained role of this fork is to:

- consume gem5-compatible architectural checkpoint state prepared from qflex
  snapshots
- consume staged microarchitectural restore artifacts prepared by QPoints
- run timing simulation on those restored checkpoints
- support the protocol, cache-restore, TLB-restore, and timing-methodology
  changes needed by this project

The broader workflow around this fork is:

1. qflex drives boot, workload bring-up, initialization, and functional
   warming on the source side
2. BXKraken-origin source state is captured as snapshots plus recorded
   microarchitectural state
3. qflex/QPoints convert that source state into a gem5-consumable checkpoint
4. this gem5 fork restores the architectural and microarchitectural state and
   performs timing simulation

This fork therefore expects checkpoint inputs and restore artifacts prepared
for it. It is not documented here as a standalone user entrypoint.

For the maintained user-facing workflow, start from:

- `../../docs/gem5_conversion/project_progress.md`
- `../../docs/gem5_conversion/qflex_cli_guide.md`
- `../README.md`

For the current bridge-layer role of QPoints, see:

- `../README.md`

Build requirements remain broadly the same as upstream gem5: a working C++
toolchain, Python, SCons, SWIG, zlib, m4, and protobuf when trace capture or
playback support is needed.

Typical project build targets in this repository are ARM Ruby timing binaries,
for example:

- `build/ARM_MESI_Two_Level/gem5.opt`
- `build/ARM_MOESI_CMP_directory/gem5.opt`

This fork also carries project-specific restore and timing changes. If you are
trying to understand why a configuration or restore path exists here, assume
first that it supports the BXKraken-to-gem5 checkpoint workflow rather than
generic upstream gem5 usage.
